    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm more interested in the legal distinction between \"frustration of contract\" and a \"force majeure\" clause in this context. Under Singapore law, if a business interruption is so severe that it renders the lease fundamentally different from what was originally agreed, would the force majeure clause typically override the common law doctrine of frustration?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Under Singapore law, the legal distinction between \"frustration of contract\" and a \"force majeure\" clause is quite significant. Frustration of contract occurs when an unforeseen event fundamentally changes the nature of the contract, making it impossible to fulfill the original terms. In such cases, the contract may be terminated, and neither party is liable for non-performance. On the other hand, a force majeure clause is a contractual provision that specifically addresses the impact of unforeseen events on the obligations of the parties.\n\nIn the context of a Singapore commercial lease agreement, if a business interruption is so severe that it renders the lease fundamentally different from what was originally agreed, the force majeure clause would typically come into play. This clause is designed to protect both parties from liability in the event of such extraordinary circumstances, allowing them to avoid penalties or obligations that would otherwise be due under the lease agreement.\n\nHowever, it's important to note that the force majeure clause and the doctrine of frustration are not mutually exclusive. In fact, they can work together to provide a comprehensive legal framework for dealing with unforeseen events. If the force majeure clause is triggered, it may prevent the application of the doctrine of frustration, as the clause specifically addresses the impact of unforeseen events on the obligations of the parties",
      "source": "target",
